{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^##|''^###|''^'\"\\\\*\\\\*' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"5:**How to read the numbers.** ✔ means I ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with three@0.180.0 and @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 and it behaved as described. Skill results are written sharp / average / clumsy, from the calibrated bots in section 17. They are simulations of a player, not a player. No mark means a starting value: tune it with the dev sliders and the balance test.\n7:**Contents:** 1 The game · 2 Originality rules · 3 Controls · 4 The balance model · 5 Programme and progress · 6 Events · 7 Screens · 8 Cast and writing · 9 Art bible · 10 Animation · 11 Audio · 12 Multiplayer · 13 Attract mode · 14 Dev mode · 15 Touch-only · 16 Architecture and build · 17 Testing · 18 Build order and cut order · 19 Done and reporting · 20 What was and wasn't verified · 21 TASK.md coverage and proof · 22 Planning-stage report\n11:## 1. The game\n13:**Pitch.** A wooden-toy wellness retreat stands on an island balanced on a single rock, and the island has been crooked for as long as anyone remembers. You arrive as a new guest, a jointed peg doll. Every morning a fussy talking spirit level named Trudy checks you in, asks something too personal and tells you your Wobble Age. Then you go to class: hold poses with a heron, do reps under a log for a walrus, step to city pop on the bandstand, catch fruit in a basket hat, stack stones on a slab over your head. Every class pours marbles into the counterweight jar, and the whole island visibly straightens, day by day, until the morning Trudy's bubble sits in the middle for the first time.\n15:**Title.** Do TASK.md's title step first, since it names your folder and file. The title is a joke on the source's name, never the name itself. Keep it in one constant (`src/data/config.js`). Nothing in the fiction depends on it: the resort is the Level Best Retreat, and the cast and events have their own names.\n17:**Scope.** A feature-complete demo: a complete, polished vertical slice with every system in place, long enough to show everything the game can do. A full pass of the programme runs about 45 minutes.\n19:**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression. **Perspective:** third person. Each event has its own framed stage camera. On the island it is a chase camera you can orbit.\n32:**Pillars. When in doubt, serve these.**\n39:**The first five minutes** (build this exact beat, it is the game's first impression):\n50:## 2. Originality rules\n61:## 3. Controls\n98:## 4. The balance model\n102:**The doll's \"balance core\"** is one Rapier dynamic body on a ball joint at the foot. Everything in the game that stands uses it. Its colliders can carry props, and those props push back on it through real contacts, so a heavy hat makes you top-heavy without any special code.\n133:**Defaults ✔:** `maxLean` 18°, `margin` 0.45, `zeta` 0.40, `ankleMax` 4.6 N·m, `reflex` 10 /s, `flailBoost` 1.9, flail lasts 0.35 s with a 2 s cooldown, fall at 45° of lean.\n135:**What those numbers do ✔:**\n140:**Gusts at the defaults ✔ (0.5 s long; these numbers are the difficulty dial):**\n152:**The load rule ✔.** Every prop adds a gravity moment δ = mass × 9.81 × height. The doll grows unstable when total δ exceeds K − G (3.3 at the default margin). A prop's mass therefore decides how many you can carry. Too heavy and the doll tips over at once: with margin 0.45, 0.06 kg fruit tipped it at the 5th or 6th catch, while 0.015 kg works out to about 13. **Keep prop masses small** (0.01 to 0.03 kg) against the doll's 1 kg.\n154:**Slow sway is what makes holding still a game ✔.** A thumb is far steadier than a body on a board, so a player who simply parks the stick on a target and leaves it scored 100% in my first test. Fast tremor barely moves the dot. The fix is slow forcing that the player has to see and counter by hand. Add it to `nx`, `nz`:\n164:**Traps I hit ✔:**\n174:**The Balance Disc.** `cob = (leanX, leanZ) / maxLean` is the dot, shown truthfully. Draw it as inline SVG and move it with transforms each frame. It is the game's signature HUD: a large circular dial with rings, a target zone that turns green when you are inside, the solid dot, a faint ring showing where your stick is asking to go, and a short inked trail of the last half second, like a seismograph needle. Diameter is about 22% of screen height, bottom left. Trudy peeks over its rim and reacts. In Group Class each player gets a smaller one (14%) along the bottom edge.\n176:**Falling.** At 45° of lean the doll becomes a ragdoll (section 10) with a clatter of wooden clacks, a quip, a rumble and a comic \"CLACK!\" word in the world. After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost. Disable the core while the ragdoll runs (`body.setEnabled(false)`). On recovery reset its pose, velocities and **your cached `leanX`, `leanZ`** ✔: if you forget, the stale lean makes the doll fall again at once, for ever. Falls are counted in the Journal as Floor Exercises.\n178:**Flail.** One button, all devices. Arms windmill for 0.35 s and the ankle gets `flailBoost` strength. Cooldown is 2 s. It looks ridiculous on purpose. It is the save that makes near-falls exciting, and it only works if you time it (see the table above). A flail costs a small form penalty in Stillness events, and the instructor comments.\n180:**Breath.** In Stillness events a breath ring pulses: 4 s growing, 4 s shrinking. Hold Breathe while it grows, release while it shrinks. Over the last full cycle, 70%+ in sync multiplies sway and tremor by 0.6 and form gain by 1.25. Out of sync does nothing bad except miss the bonus. Accessibility toggle: auto-breathe. It is worth a lot ✔: perfectly in sync, Poses Tier 1 goes 95→100 / 66→91 / 54→78, and The Cairn Tier 2 goes from 5.3 / 4.8 / 2 stones of 8 to 8 / 8 / 6.3.\n182:**Difficulty** (Settings): Gentle, Standard (default), Honest. It multiplies `ankleMax` by 1.25 / 1 / 0.9, disturbances by 0.65 / 1 / 1.2 and rhythm windows by 1.4 / 1 / 0.8.\n186:## 5. Programme and progress\n188:**Days are game days, not calendar days.** A day ends when you go to bed. The real date is only used for absence jokes and calendar stamps.\n201:**A day's flow:** morning line → personal question (Yes/No) → Check-In → Wobble Age reveal → Today's Plan (cards in any order: walk there or pick a card to go directly) → each class → Results → marbles → the island comes up a notch → extra Free Play if wanted → the bed hut. Press Use, the screen inks out, a stamp lands on the calendar, the next dawn. Palettes follow the day's steps (dawn, noon, golden, dusk), unless an event pins its own (Step Class at dusk, The Grand Hold at sunset).\n203:**The Check-In** (about 90 s) takes place on a round mat with Trudy. Three tests, each one screen with one big instruction. Each scores 0 to 1 (clamped):\n210:**Wobble Age.** `age = round(18 + 81 × (1 − mean)^1.3 + handicap)`, clamped to 18..99. The handicap is a comic head start that shrinks each day: +24, +16, +8, +3, then 0. So the number always falls, and that is what makes coming back feel good. With unchanged skill, Day 1 to Day 5 ✔: 56→32 / 66→42 / 86→62. The reveal is the comic set piece: lights dim, drum roll, numbers spin on a wooden counter, sting, Trudy's line from a band of lines by age. Stored per day and drawn as the Journal's graph. It never asks your real age (\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\").\n212:**Marbles.** After each class: 2 per minute of active play (min 2) + medal bonus (1, 2, 3, 5) + 2 for a first clear. Each Check-In gives 1. They pour physically into the jar on the island, as real Rapier balls: a 200-marble pour runs fine (✔ 0.23 ms per step; 500 is 0.67 ms). Marble colour shows the medal. Thresholds unlock hats and paints (`src/data/unlocks.js`: 6, 14, 24, 36, 50, 60 marbles). A full programme pays about 15, 37, 59, 81, 103 by the end of each day, or 61 in total with all Tier B events cut, so everything unlocks either way ✔.\n214:**The island's tilt depends on progress, not skill.** Rest tilt = 9° × (1 − completed programme steps / total steps), so nobody can be stuck. Each step lowers it with a long wooden creak, all loose props shuffling and Trudy reacting. The last notch happens inside The Grand Hold.\n216:**Medals** come from form (0 to 100): ≥40 Adequate, ≥65 Pleasant, ≥85 Exemplary, ≥97 Suspicious. Below 40 is still a pass (\"Attended\"). Section 6 gives an event's form where it has its own formula. Everywhere else, form = 100 × raw / `par`, with `par` set per tier so the sharp bot lands 88 to 95 (section 17).\n218:**Tiers.** Every event has three: Gentle, Honest, Unreasonable. Tier 2 opens with a Pleasant on Tier 1, Tier 3 with an Exemplary on Tier 2. Days 1 to 5 use Tier 1. Maintenance days use the highest tier opened. Free Play offers all open tiers.\n220:**Free Play** opens each event once it has been met in the programme. Walk to its pavilion, or use the menu.\n222:**The ending.** The Grand Hold (section 6) ends with the island level. Trudy's bubble centres for the first time. She has only ever seen the world at nine degrees. \"Oh. So that's what the middle looks like.\" Silence, then the marbles flare, the lighthouse lights, and the credits roll over a slow pan of everything you straightened. Then: \"It's quite flat, isn't it? ...Same time tomorrow?\" Save the \"levelled\" state, unlock Free Play for everything, and carry on into Maintenance days.\n224:**Profiles.** Up to four, each with a name, paint, hat, days, ages, medals, marbles and counts. Name is a spinner over generated names (no text entry; `src/data/names.js`). Save JSON in `localStorage` with a version number, read inside try/catch, and fall back to memory if storage is unavailable.\n228:## 6. Events\n230:### Common rules\n232:**What you can and can't do.** Every Intro Card and the How to Play page say what you can do here and, in one line, what you can't: \"You can't fall in this one.\" \"Your feet don't matter here.\" \"You can't lose this; you can only do it badly.\" A player should never have to guess whether a thing is possible.\n234:**Flow.** Intro Card (name, one sentence, what you can and can't do, one diagram, this device's prompts; Use to begin) → instructor counts 3-2-1 → play → whistle or bell → Results (ink-stamp medal, one big number, one line of stats, a line of dialogue, retry or continue) → marbles in the programme.\n236:**Lanes.** Every event is written for an array of lanes from the first line: `for (const lane of this.lanes)`. A lane is `{ id, kind: 'local' | 'bot' | 'ghost', input, doll, balancer, props, score, name, colour }`. Solo is one lane. Group Class is up to four local lanes. A hub session is one local lane plus ghost lanes. Attract mode uses bot lanes. Lanes sit 3.2 m apart on x. Each has its own collision bits. Ghost lanes have no physics at all (section 12). The camera frames all lanes in Group Class, and your own lane with neighbours partly visible in a session. Field of view 38° ✔ (the look tests used it). Pull back to fit lanes: `d = max(base, (spread / 2 + 2.5) / tan(hfov / 2))`. Chase camera: about (0, 3.4, 9) behind the doll, damped at 6 /s.\n238:**Each event provides:** `buildSet()`, `start()`, `fixed(dt)`, `frame(dt)`, `laneResult(i)`, `botInput(i, t, skill)`, `dispose()`, plus static `id, title, discipline, instructor, tiers, couch, session, sessionConfig`. A fresh Rapier world per event, freed in `dispose()`: no state leaks. Gameplay randomness comes only from `rng('event-id')` (section 16).\n240:**Non-standing poses reuse the balance core:** in Plank & Perch, Still Water and Tug of Peace the core stays upright and invisible, and the visible doll plays the pose and rocks with it. Nothing in the rules knows the difference.\n242:### STILLNESS, with Ms. Stillwater\n244:**Poses** (Sun Deck, a round teak deck on stilts over the water; low three-quarter camera, heron beside you)\n251:**The Cairn** (Quarry Terrace, pale stone over the cliff; side three-quarter camera)\n258:**Still Water** (Lily Pond; high three-quarter camera over the water)\n263:### STRENGTH, with Coach Bolster\n265:**Log Reps** (The Yard; front three-quarter camera, Coach beside you shouting encouragement)\n272:**Plank & Perch** (The Beam, on the pier; side camera)\n277:**Tug of Peace** (The Beach; side camera; versus)\n283:### CARDIO, with Coach Bolster and the class\n285:**Step Class** (The Bandstand at dusk; front camera, class of eight dolls behind you following your moves)\n293:**Plate Spinners** (The Circus Green; high front camera)\n300:**Island Loop** (the island's ring road; chase camera)\n305:### BALANCE PLAY, with Trudy\n307:**Fruit Hat** (end of the pier; barge offshore firing fruit; camera low from the sea side) — **build this event first. It is the game's soul and the proof of the model.**\n314:**Room Service** (a long boardwalk out to a far cabana; chase camera from behind and above)\n320:**Springboard** (the toy-block dive tower at the end of the pier; side camera, slow-mo side replay)\n329:**Tippy Raft** (a round raft at sea; high wide camera; party event) — the one event where players physically fight, and Group Class's reason to exist.\n334:### THE FINALE\n336:**The Grand Hold** (lighthouse gallery at sunset; camera circles slowly, the whole cast and island below)\n341:### Island physics (hub, Island Loop, Tippy Raft, finale) ✔\n343:**Do not move the island. Tilt gravity.** Rapier sees a fixed flat disc; the visual island group rotates; gravity is set to match each step. All the physics stays in island-local coordinates, with no moving-platform problems to solve. Up to 11° of tilt, a walker's height stayed within 0.004 m, and 60 props slid to the low side with none lost. (A kinematic platform also worked, but it is more to manage: I only took it to 1.8° and props rolled off.)\n361:## 7. Screens\n363:**The 10-foot rules.** All UI is a DOM overlay over the canvas. Lowest text size is 30 px at 1080p; body text 36+; titles huge. Keep a 5% safe margin. At most six choices on a screen, not counting a page switcher. Longer lists are split into pages. One idea per screen. No tables, no scrolling lists of data, no small print except on About, which is paged. Every focusable thing has a thick, bouncing, inked focus state, and hover moves that focus. Default font: Fredoka (section 16). UI elements look like paper tags and wooden signs with inked borders, each slightly rotated, never a web form.\n389:## 8. Cast and writing\n391:**Writing style.** Short sentences. Polite. Second person. Chirpy and calm, with something slightly off. Instructions are plain and exact, then a small, sincere, odd aside. Praise is mild and specific. Every failure gets a line that makes it fine. The joke is the straight face. Write at least 200 Trudy lines, 60 for each instructor, 60 for guests, in `src/data/dialogue.js`, by situation, picked without repeats.\n393:**Trudy, Head of Alignment.** A chunky spirit level on two stubby pegs. Her bubble is her eye: it slides to the high side of the world, and to wherever she is feeling. She has been crooked with the island since she was installed and thinks of this as normal. Fussy aunt energy. Keeps count of everything and insists she isn't. She gets attached to you and would die before saying so.\n406:**Ms. Stillwater, Stillness.** A tall heron on one leg. Half-closed eyes. A voice like a slow door. She has never blinked on camera. She notices everything and is not bothered by any of it.\n414:**Coach Bolster, Strength and Cardio.** A vast walrus in a sweatband with a whistle he always forgets to blow. Loves you unconditionally and loudly. Bad at counting, on purpose.\n421:**Guests,** all peg dolls in different paint and hats. They wander, watch your classes and react: Gentle Margaret, Retired Kevin, Both Sandras (two dolls, always together, always agreeing), Unsteady Paul, Young Gerald (92). **Gary** is a gull. He steals things and lands on you, turning up in several events. In the finale he sits on your head and Trudy says, \"He's part of it now.\" Nobody ever mentions that everyone is a wooden toy.\n425:## 9. Art bible\n429:**Base style.** TASK.md says to use the engine's native style as the base. Three.js has no house style and no asset library, so the base is its own toolkit: node materials, lights, shadows and TSL post-processing, with its built-in geometries and addons for generic props. Everything that makes the game feel like itself (the cast, the island, the jar, every event's props) is a new asset made in code.\n431:**Detail.** The performance budget is unlimited, per TASK.md. Fill the sets: lots of guests, loose props, toy trees, bunting, particles, extra lights where a scene wants them. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope.\n433:**Palette ✔ (all four rendered and checked).** Ink `#1A1420` on everything. Seat colours are the hub's: `#E8443A`, `#3B82F6`, `#22C55E`, `#F5B700`.\n444:**Materials ✔.** Everything solid is cel-shaded: `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })` with a 3-step `DataTexture` ramp `[96, 178, 255]`, `NearestFilter`, `NoColorSpace`. `renderer.toneMapping = THREE.NoToneMapping` keeps the palette true. Sky and sea use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial` with a TSL `colorNode`. Anything that should get a hull outline must be a toon material.\n446:**Shadows ✔.** `renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true`, `PCFShadowMap`, one sun with a 2048 map, `shadow.bias = -0.0006`, `shadow.normalBias = 0.035`. Without those two, the toon surfaces are covered in acne.\n448:**The ink recipe ✔** (runs on WebGPU and on the WebGL2 fallback, tested in Chromium and WebKit):\n492:**Sea ✔.** `length(positionWorld.xz)` into a `sin` with a small domain warp and `time`, then `step(0.86, wave)` between `sea` and `seaHi` for hard bands, mixed to `skyLow` by distance from the camera (60 to 260 units). Add a foam ring near the island by distance from its centre, plus rings that follow the tilt.\n494:**Sky ✔.** Inverted sphere radius 600, `mix` of the three colours on the view direction's y (`smoothstep(0, .14)` and `smoothstep(.1, .5)`). Add inked toon clouds as flattened spheres that drift.\n496:**Model recipes ✔ (all primitives, all parented to named joints).** Build in code from `SphereGeometry` (scaled), `CapsuleGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry`, `ConeGeometry`, `TorusGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`, `TubeGeometry` and `RoundedBoxGeometry` (from `three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js`).\n523:## 10. Animation\n532:**Ragdoll from the rig ✔ (10 bodies, stable).** When a doll falls, create one dynamic body per segment at the segment's world transform (body origin at the joint, collider offset along the segment). Reparent the segment's visual to the scene (`scene.attach`) and copy each body's transform to it each step. Use spherical joints with `setContactsEnabled(false)` and the ragdoll collision bits. Tested: capsules torso (hh .2, r .22, m .45), head (.02, .2, .15), upper arm (.1, .07, .05), lower arm (.1, .07, .04), upper leg (.13, .09, .08), lower leg (.13, .08, .06), linear damping .05, angular damping .6, friction .7, restitution .15. Max speed 7 m/s, no NaN, settles in about 5.5 s; end it at `recoverTime`. Start it with the core's velocity and a small impulse on the head. On recovery, `attach` the segments back and restore their local transforms.\n538:## 11. Audio\n542:**Mix.** `buses (music, sfx, voice) → master compressor → destination`. Settings 0 to 100. **Music defaults to 10** (gain 0.10), sound 80, voice 80. Start the context on the first gesture, including pad input (section 3); before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\n544:**What I could check without ears ✔** (TASK.md's stand-in: render it and read the signal). I wrote the FM e-piano, bass, foot click and voices from this section and rendered them with `OfflineAudioContext`. All 21 notes of the title motif came out at the written pitch class (19 exact; two read an octave low by autocorrelation, likely the detector). With backing, the peak is −2.1 dB with nothing clipped. **With music at 10 and sound at 80, a foot click stands +17.6 dB above the music just before it (weakest +7 dB),** so Step Class's beat is readable. In a real-time run an `AnalyserNode` on the music bus heard the motif, at bus gain .10.\n546:**Music engine.** Look-ahead scheduler (25 ms tick, 150 ms ahead) on `currentTime`. Reverb from a `ConvolverNode` with a generated noise-decay impulse. Tracks are data: `{ bpm, swing, chords per bar, parts }` in `src/audio/tracks.js`. Instruments: FM electric piano (sine carrier with a 14:1 tine and a 1:1 body, fast index decay), slap bass (saw through an enveloped low-pass plus a click), marimba (sine plus 4× partial, fast decay), vibes (sine plus 4× partial and tremolo), pad (three detuned saws, low-pass, slow attack), FM brass stab, breathy flute (sine plus band-passed noise), kick (sine sweep 120→45 Hz), gated snare (noise into short reverb), brushes and shaker (filtered noise), woodblock, rim.\n548:**Score.** Original smooth city-pop and bossa, nothing like the source's music.\n561:**Title theme motif** (use as written; MIDI, on a 16-step bar, `[step, note, length]`):\n568:**Out-of-tune island.** While the rest tilt is above zero, detune every music note by `tilt° × 1.6 × sin(t × 0.7)` cents. At level, it is exactly in tune. The player will feel this before they notice it.\n570:**Voices.** Animalese-style gibberish. Each line is spoken from its own text: strip it to letters, walk the letters at the character's rate, and for each syllable-group fire a short blip: a pulse or saw oscillator at the character's pitch with slight random drift, through two band-pass filters at formants picked by vowel (a, e, i, o, u, each with a set F1 and F2), 60 ms envelope; consonants add a very short noise tick. Pitch rises on `?`, jumps up on `!`, falls at the end of a sentence. Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\"Wobba-la!\", \"Oof-ah.\", \"Nee nee!\"). The text types out in time. **Balance the voices ✔:** at equal settings Trudy's narrow formant filters peaked at +1 dB (clipping, rms −20 dB), while Ms. Stillwater and Coach Bolster sat near −30 dB rms. Give each voice its own gain so all peak around −6 dB. Spaces and punctuation bring the real rate down to about 12.7, 5.5 and 8.7 blips a second for the rates below.\n580:**SFX** (`src/audio/sfx.js`, all parameterised, with small random variation): wood clack (three resonant noise bursts, tuned per limb), toy spring (rising sine with vibrato), splash (noise with falling low-pass, big or small), marble drop and roll (short high pings, pitch random, more as the jar fills), plate hum (sine following speed), plate crash, fruit thud, urchin spike, whistle, bell, breath in and out (filtered noise), wind gust (rising band-passed noise), wooden creak (for the island: slow saw through a resonant filter), UI move, confirm and back (woodblock tones), stamp thud, drum roll, foot click.\n584:## 12. Multiplayer\n586:**Two modes, both required.** On one screen by itself (Group Class, as the hub's own docs say couch play stays the game's own, up to four controllers), and across devices in an AI Hub session. Both use the lane system, so they cost little beyond it.\n588:### Group Class: on its own, 1-4 on one TV\n594:### AI Hub session: ghost mode, 1-4 devices\n598:**Why ghost.** Every copy runs its own physics, and the others appear as dolls in the next lanes doing the same class: it looks exactly like a fitness class. It is robust to latency, because nobody's balance depends on the network.\n600:**Head of the page, in this order** (the hub's check reads `name` then `content`, so keep that attribute order):\n616:**The boot path** (first statements of `main.js`, before anything heavy). Wrap it in an async function: an `iife` build rejects top-level `await`.\n629:**In `runSession(info)`:**\n638:**Session rules to keep:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; no other network; no chat, emotes or custom names (so no wave button in a session); names only from the hub; all gameplay random numbers from `AIHub.rng(label)`.\n640:**On its own:** `AIHub.active` is false and the game behaves as if the hub didn't exist. Pressing Enter and Space repeatedly on the title must be harmless and leave something drawn: the hub's check does exactly this.\n644:## 13. Attract mode\n648:**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\n652:## 14. Dev mode\n656:**Tools:** jump to any event and tier; jump to any day or step; skip to Results or to the Ending; god mode (no falls); time scale 0.1× to 4×; single-step; free camera; physics debug lines (`world.debugRender()` into a `LineSegments`); stats (fps, step ms, bodies, draw calls); input monitor (both sticks, triggers, last device); force a gust, a fall, a fruit or a stone; play any dialogue line, music track or sting; set Wobble Age; add marbles; unlock all; toggle ink and pick palette; let the bot play; reset save; copy tuning as JSON (to the clipboard, falling back to a large on-screen text so nothing needs a dialog).\n658:**Live sliders with Reset to Defaults** (`src/data/tuning.js`: each entry `{ group, label, min, max, step, default }`; the whole game reads tuning live):\n665:**For later designers.** TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable with normal designer tools. So events' tiers, poses, charts, dialogue, days, unlocks, palettes and tuning are all plain data tables in `src/data/`. A designer can add a tier, a day or a line of dialogue without touching systems, and test it from Dev Mode. Keep it that way.\n669:## 15. Touch-only devices\n671:**Detect with media queries, not `maxTouchPoints`:** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`. Playwright's iPhone profile reports `maxTouchPoints` 0 ✔.\n677:## 16. Architecture and build\n679:**Where things go.** Keep all your dev files in the subfolder TASK.md names (your dev folder). Develop separate modules there, and bundle them into the single HTML file in the root, named as TASK.md says, when you're done. TASK.md allows external CDN dependencies; this plan inlines everything instead, so the file works offline and inside the hub.\n681:**Stack.** `three@0.180.0` (import only from `three/webgpu` and `three/tsl`; addons from `three/addons/...` work ✔), `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/fredoka` (SIL OFL; put its name and licence in the credits). Web Audio and the Gamepad API directly. `npm i` these in the dev folder ✔; Playwright finds the browsers already here.\n705:**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.25) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 30 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`. That keeps the game on real time down to 4 fps ✔: with a 0.1 s cap at this computer's 6 fps, everything including attract's 10 seconds ran slow. Input polls inside the fixed tick. **All game logic and UI flow run on sim time, never `setTimeout`,** via a `game.after(sec, fn)` scheduler and generator scripts. This is what makes a whole-game test take a minute. Use generators for sequences: `function* dayOne(d) { yield d.say('trudy', 'intro.step_on'); yield d.wait(1); ... }`, ticked by `src/core/script.js`.\n707:**Random numbers.** `src/core/rng.js` is the only file that may touch randomness. `rng(label)` returns `AIHub.rng(label)` in a session and a mulberry32 stream outside one. Cosmetics use a private stream. Never call `Math.random` directly. In a session the client seeds it for fairness, and stray draws don't matter, but nothing should depend on it.\n709:**Renderer ✔.** `new THREE.WebGPURenderer({ antialias: true })`, `await renderer.init()`, then check `renderer.backend.isWebGPUBackend`. It falls back to WebGL2 by itself when there's no adapter ✔ (a harmless warning). Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Watch `renderer.backend.device.lost` and record it, because a lost device gives you no error and a loop that seems to run (section 17). Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer, with flags that really render, a simple scene runs at about 6 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\n711:**Rapier ✔.** `import RAPIER from '@dimforge/rapier3d-compat'; await RAPIER.init();`. It works inlined with no extra file. It logs one harmless warning about deprecated init parameters. Fixed step: `world.timestep = 1/120`. Free each world with `world.free()`.\n713:**Build ✔.** One self-contained HTML with no external requests.\n733:**Fresh world per event.** Events get a new Rapier world and a new `THREE.Group`, added under one scene that also holds sky, sea and lights. The island is hidden during events, or kept far in the background as scenery where a set wants it. Transitions hide the swap with the ink wipe.\n737:## 17. Testing\n741:**Sharing this computer (TASK.md).** Other agents run here at the same time.\n749:**Stand-ins for what this computer doesn't have.**\n760:**The command.** `node tools/test.mjs [group ...]`. Groups: `unit`, `flow`, `events`, `balance`, `input`, `audio`, `hub`, `ui`, `full`. It prints each failure with its reason, then one line: `N passed, M failed`. Rerun only the group your change touches.\n762:**Browser flags ✔ (use this set for anything that needs the renderer):**\n773:**Test hooks.** With `?test=1`, the game exposes `window.__TEST__`:\n780:**Input stubs.** Replace `navigator.getGamepads` with `add_init_script`/`addInitScript`, returning `{ connected: true, mapping: 'standard', axes: [...], buttons: [{ pressed, value }, ...], vibrationActuator: { playEffect() {} } }` from `window.__pads`. Use `page.keyboard` and `page.mouse` for the rest. When a bot stops, zero the stub: a stick left pushed scrolls every menu ✔.\n782:**The bots ✔ (the human model every skill number in this plan came from).** A delayed correction: the bot sees the dot `delay` seconds ago and sets `wish = goal + gain × (goal − dot) + noise × sin(...)`. Do not raise gain under heavy load: it oscillates and tips, which is a bot failure and not a game bug.\n790:**Keep a tally.** TASK.md asks how many issues testing caught and how many were critical. Append one line to `notes/issues.md` each time a test catches a real issue, as you go. Counting afterwards doesn't work.\n792:**Tests to write.**\n810:**Art pass, by eye, at the end.** `tools/shots.mjs` with `RENDER` at 1920×1080: title, island on Day 1 and Day 5, each event mid-play, results, jar, Age Reveal, Group Class with four dolls, Dev Mode, About. Look for lost legibility at 10 feet, sunk characters, messy ink.\n814:## 18. Build order and cut order\n829:**Cut order if you run short. Cut whole things, never stub them, and leave no trace (TASK.md: no stubs, no placeholders).** First Island Loop, then Tug of Peace, then Plank & Perch, then Still Water, then Tier 3 of each event (leaving a two-tier ladder), then Tier 2. Do not cut: Fruit Hat, Poses, Check-In, Trudy, the tilting island, the jar, the finale, Attract, Dev Mode, Group Class, the hub session, glyphs, audio. The day table builds from the registry, so cutting an event needs no other change.\n833:## 19. Done and reporting\n835:**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features: the demo is complete. Before you finish, check every line:\n850:**Three places, the same content.** TASK.md calls this critical: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in **your chat reply** and an **in-game About/Credits screen,** and into **the exit-interview file** beside the HTML. These are your own answers from your own build. Nothing in this plan answers them for you, and section 22 is only an example. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n852:**The last thing you do, in this order:**\n861:**The About/Credits screen.** Reached from the main menu. It follows the 10-foot rules: large type, one page at a time, moved with left and right or clicks, never a scrolling wall of text. It is also the one screen where long answers may take several pages: split them rather than shrink them.\n876:**Your final chat reply** has this shape, with your own answers:\n879:## Source Information\n881:## Testing Feedback\n883:## Engine Feedback\n885:## Prompt Feedback\n889:**Source Information.** Field by field:\n908:**Testing Feedback,** answered in order (`notes/issues.md` gives you the counts):\n914:**Engine Feedback,** answered in order:\n922:**Prompt Feedback,** answered in order:\n928:**The exit-interview file** is one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape, plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses: `sourceInformation` (`date, source, plan, genre, perspective, tech, artDirection, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage`), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback`, `promptFeedback` (arrays, answers in order), and `aiHub`:\n945:## 20. What was and wasn't verified\n947:**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above):** versions exist on npm and jsDelivr; single-file esbuild build from `file://`; WebGPU in Chromium with SwiftShader, and which flags really render; WebGL2 fallback in Chromium and WebKit; `toonOutlinePass` with MRT and the custom ink pass; instanced toon meshes; glass; shadow settings; the look of the cast and all four palettes; client hash; balance model; torque limit; gusts and flail; island tilt; marble pours; ragdoll; Rapier's crash conditions and a soak of every removal path; touch media queries; Fredoka's licence and file names.\n949:**Every event's numbers were simulated with the bots,** on real Rapier where physics matter: Fruit Hat, Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Room Service, Springboard, Island Loop, The Grand Hold and Step Class on the real balance core, Tippy Raft, Plate Spinners from a moving stick vector, and the Check-In. Keys were simulated against the stick for Poses, Still Water, Fruit Hat, Room Service and Plate Spinners. This overturned my first numbers for Plate Spinners, Tug of Peace, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Room Service, Tippy Raft, Lean Match, The Cairn's top tier, Step Class's lean notes and the keyboard model. Expect the same when you build: **measure before you trust a number.**\n951:**An integration prototype.** Separate checks don't prove the parts work together, so I built one event through everything, from this document: its build recipe, its code blocks extracted directly from this file, a rig with `AnimationMixer` clips, ragdoll from the rig, Fruit Hat written for lanes, all three input devices, live prompts with both glyph sets, menus, pause, Dev Mode sliders with reset, attract mode, touch-only, an About screen fed from a JSON file, audio buses with the title motif, and a hub session with its own card, ghost lanes and a timeline on the hub's clock. Then I tested it the way section 17 says, with one command: **209 passed, 0 failed,** with real rendering, including all 16 harness checks in each of five hub scenarios (1, 2 and 4 players, bad network, host leaving, short limit), a wall-clock attract, three touch profiles, and the 1080p layout audit. That took about 360 lines of game code and 170 of tests, and came to a 3.1 MB single file. It is deleted now; everything it taught is in this plan. It found problems no separate check had shown: the lost device, the parked mouse stealing focus, both mouse buttons never registering together, silent controller play, the fall loop, lost quick taps, and slow time at low frame rates.\n953:**Not tested. TASK.md says anything needing the real thing \"is checked as far as it can be here, never required\", and these are as far as it goes:**\n961:## 21. TASK.md coverage and proof\n1026:## 22. Planning-stage report\n1030:**Source Information.** Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:05. Source: Wii Fit. Plan: none (this stage wrote it). Genre and Perspective: physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island. Tech: Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon and ink), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0. Art Direction: cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code. Animation: `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls (prototyped with test clips). Audio: code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices (motif, voices and click prototyped). Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 on one screen. Testing: as much headless testing as wanted, with scripts that play and check state; one command printing only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: simulations with three bot skills, and the prototype's suite. Research: the hub's protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries of Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Iwata Asks. Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux. Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max. Usage: added on import.\n1032:**Testing Feedback**\n1038:**Engine Feedback** (Three.js and Rapier, from the prototypes)\n1046:**Prompt Feedback**\n1052:**The exit-interview file's shape,** filled in for this stage. It parses, and its keys match TASK.md's exactly. Replace every value with your own. `name` and `tagline` are \"unknown\" here only because each builder picks its own title.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Director's Plan\n\nBuild the game from this document. Nobody can answer questions, so where the plan is silent, decide and move on. TASK.md stays the authority on what to deliver, how it is named, what to report and how you may test. This plan decides what the game is and how it is built. Paths are relative to your dev folder.\n\n**How to read the numbers.** ✔ means I ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with three@0.180.0 and @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 and it behaved as described. Skill results are written sharp / average / clumsy, from the calibrated bots in section 17. They are simulations of a player, not a player. No mark means a starting value: tune it with the dev sliders and the balance test.\n\n**Contents:** 1 The game · 2 Originality rules · 3 Controls · 4 The balance model · 5 Programme and progress · 6 Events · 7 Screens · 8 Cast and writing · 9 Art bible · 10 Animation · 11 Audio · 12 Multiplayer · 13 Attract mode · 14 Dev mode · 15 Touch-only · 16 Architecture and build · 17 Testing · 18 Build order and cut order · 19 Done and reporting · 20 What was and wasn't verified · 21 TASK.md coverage and proof · 22 Planning-stage report\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** A wooden-toy wellness retreat stands on an island balanced on a single rock, and the island has been crooked for as long as anyone remembers. You arrive as a new guest, a jointed peg doll. Every morning a fussy talking spirit level named Trudy checks you in, asks something too personal and tells you your Wobble Age. Then you go to class: hold poses with a heron, do reps under a log for a walrus, step to city pop on the bandstand, catch fruit in a basket hat, stack stones on a slab over your head. Every class pours marbles into the counterweight jar, and the whole island visibly straightens, day by day, until the morning Trudy's bubble sits in the middle for the first time.\n\n**Title.** Do TASK.md's title step first, since it names your folder and file. The title is a joke on the source's name, never the name itself. Keep it in one constant (`src/data/config.js`). Nothing in the fiction depends on it: the resort is the Level Best Retreat, and the cast and events have their own names.\n\n**Scope.** A feature-complete demo: a complete, polished vertical slice with every system in place, long enough to show everything the game can do. A full pass of the programme runs about 45 minutes.\n\n**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression. **Perspective:** third person. Each event has its own framed stage camera. On the island it is a chase camera you can orbit.\n\n| What makes the source itself | Our version |\n|---|---|\n| You see your own balance as a dot in a target | The Balance Disc, in every event, driven by real physics |\n| A rectangle given a personality, who asks awkward questions and notices when you skip days | Trudy, a talking spirit level whose bubble is her eye |\n| Calm, slightly uncanny instructors; \"the trainer notices when you slack off\" | Ms. Stillwater (a heron) and Coach Bolster (a walrus), who both notice |\n| A daily test that hands you an embarrassing age | The Check-In and your Wobble Age |\n| Four kinds of training | Stillness, Strength, Cardio, Balance Play |\n| Minutes trained fill a bank that opens new things | Marbles pour into a glass jar, physically |\n| The family takes turns and laughs at each other | Group Class on one TV; an AI Hub class across devices |\n| Absurd little games between the earnest ones | Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft |\n\n**Pillars. When in doubt, serve these.**\n1. **One verb: shift your weight.** Every event reads the same Balance Dot from the same physics. Learn it once and you can play everything.\n2. **Physics tells the jokes.** Falling is the best moment in the game: clattering wooden limbs, a straight-faced line from Trudy, a spring back up. Failure is never punishing.\n3. **A gentle ritual.** Check in, train, watch the marbles, go to bed. No skill gate ever stops the story.\n4. **The world is the progress bar.** The lighthouse leans 9° on Day 1 and stands straight on the last day. Loose props slide to the low side. The music is slightly out of tune until the island is level.\n5. **The room laughs.** It is played on a TV at 10 feet, and it should be funny to watch.\n\n**The first five minutes** (build this exact beat, it is the game's first impression):\n1. Title screen at dawn. The crooked island fills the frame. The title's letters stand on a wooden beam that tilts with the island, and they slide a little. After 10 idle seconds, attract mode starts.\n2. Press to start. \"Who's here today?\" Four empty mats. Pick one. The name spinner offers a generated name (\"Gentle Margaret\"); reroll or accept. Pick a paint colour. Under a minute.\n3. You drop onto the island from above, clatter, spring up. Trudy hops over, bubble sliding to the high side. \"Oh! A person. Please step on. Not on me. On the island.\"\n4. Trudy asks you to lean left. As you do, **the whole island tips under you** and every orange on the island rolls. No menu has taught this; the world did. Lean right, then forward. \"I can feel that, by the way.\"\n5. First Check-In. Three short tests. The drum roll. \"Your Wobble Age is 74. Some of my favourite people are 74. Mostly the ones sitting down.\"\n6. Walk to the Sun Deck (or pick it from Today's Plan) for your first pose with Ms. Stillwater, then Fruit Hat on the pier, where you get greedy, fall over, and laugh.\n7. Marbles pour into the jar. The island creaks and comes up one notch. The lighthouse is visibly straighter. Bed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Originality rules\n\nThis is an original game in the source's genre, with similar kinds of mechanics. It is not a recreation.\n\n- **Never show** the source's name on screen (outside the title's joke and the About screen's Source line), nor use its product or feature names (balance board, the words it uses for its test, age or bank), its characters, its avatar style, its music or its look. No white-and-pale-blue gym, no plain white-clad instructor, no Mii-style avatars (ours are jointed wooden peg dolls), no talking board, no piggy bank.\n- **Never copy its activities.** No ski jump, slalom, soccer heading, table tilt with balls and holes, tightrope, hula hoop, penguin slide, river bubble, snowboard or boxing. The events in section 6 replace them with our own ideas. Add new ones in that spirit, not from that list.\n- **Tone guard.** Trudy teases posture, knees, commitment and attendance. **Nothing in the game comments on body weight, body shape or food.** There is no scale, no BMI and no weight number. \"Weight\" only ever means where you are leaning. No real health claims.\n- All art, music, text and voices are made for this game, in code.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Controls\n\nDesign for a PS5 DualSense, or any pad the browser reports with `mapping === 'standard'`, on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse play the same game on the same screens, with no separate desktop layout, and they should feel native rather than like stand-ins. Weight comes from a stick on a controller and from the pointer with a mouse. Both are analog. **Stick and mouse are always screen-relative:** up is away from the camera, right is screen right. Rotate the input by the camera's yaw before using it.\n\n| Verb | Controller (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys (see below); C recentres | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\n| Left foot | L2, button 6, analog `.value` | Q | Left button |\n| Right foot | R2, button 7, analog `.value` | E | Right button |\n| Hop / both feet | Cross, button 0 | Space | Both buttons |\n| Flail | Square, button 2 | F | Middle button |\n| Breathe in (hold) | R1, button 5 | Left Shift | |\n| Ask Trudy (island) | Triangle, button 3 | T | |\n| Look (island only) | Right stick, axes 2 and 3 | | Hold right button and drag; wheel zooms |\n| Use / confirm | Cross | Enter or E (island), Enter or Space (menus) | Left click |\n| Back | Circle, button 1 | Esc or Backspace | Right click (menus only; suppress the context menu) |\n| Pause | Options, button 9 | Esc | |\n| Menu move | D-pad or left stick | Arrow keys or W A S D | Hover moves the focus |\n\n- **No pointer lock is needed**, which keeps the game simple inside the hub's frames. Hide the cursor during events when the mouse is in use: the faint ring on the Balance Disc shows where it is asking you to go.\n- **Which device moves your weight:** whichever was used last. With a pad, ignore the pointer. With keyboard and mouse, keys win while a weight key is down, otherwise the pointer, once it has moved. A resting mouse never fights a resting stick.\n- **Keys in balance events ✔: they move the asking ring, and it stays where you leave it.** Holding a key moves the ring at 1.6 units/s; releasing leaves it there, and C recentres. The ring must be clearly visible on the Balance Disc, because a player steering by keys watches the ring, not the dot. I tested three models with a person-like bot. Keys that spring back on release needed 9.6 presses a second just to hold a pose. A tap-then-accelerate model scored worst (Poses Tier 1: 68 / 75 / 38). This one holds up against the stick: Poses Tier 1 98 / 62 / 54 (stick 100 / 76 / 63), Tier 2 86 / 58 / 34 (90 / 64 / 53), Still Water Tier 1 100 / 85 / 28 (100 / 93 / 24), Fruit Hat Tier 1 21 / 22 / 20 banked with no falls (28 / 33 / 28), Room Service Tier 1 all five glasses. Use a move speed of about 1.6 and don't add acceleration.\n- **Keys when you're moving, not balancing: direct.** Walking on the island, Tippy Raft, steering in Island Loop and spinning in Springboard's air use the raw key direction, as PC players expect. Plate Spinners has its own scheme ✔, below.\n- **Foot keys** ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\n- **Latch presses ✔.** A key or mouse button pressed and released between two input polls must still count, or quick taps vanish at low frame rates. Normalise key codes too: the hub's harness sends synthetic events whose `code` is just `a`, so accept `a` and `KeyA` alike.\n- **Read mouse buttons from the `buttons` bitmask ✔.** Pointer Events don't fire `pointerdown` for a second button while one is already held, so with left and right as the two feet, \"both buttons\" (Hop, Log Reps, Springboard) never registers if you only listen for `pointerdown`. Read `e.buttons` on down, up and move: bit 1 is left, 2 is right, 4 is middle.\n- **Hover means the pointer moved ✔.** Move focus only on a `pointermove` whose coordinates changed. A browser also fires `pointerenter` when a menu appears under a parked mouse, and that made focus jump by itself under a controller player.\n- **On the island,** a hovered thing (pavilion, jar, board, Trudy) gets a thick outline and a label. Left click walks there and uses it. WASD and E work without the mouse. Hit-test by projecting each hotspot's anchor to screen and taking the nearest within a radius. It is simple and robust.\n- **Prompts follow the last input.** Track `lastDevice` (`pad` or `kbm`): a pad counts when a stick passes 0.25 or a button is pressed; keyboard and mouse count on keydown, mousedown or 4+ px of movement. Prompts re-render the same frame. A Settings option switches pad glyphs between PlayStation shapes and Xbox letters (A B X Y, LT RT LB RB). Keyboard prompts show keycaps, mouse prompts show a mouse with the button lit. Draw all glyphs as inline SVG in code. Every glyph element carries a `data-glyph` attribute that tests can read.\n- **On the island,** Cross and E use the focused thing if there is one; otherwise Cross and Space hop.\n- **Create + Options belongs to the hub.** Ignore Options while Create (button 8) is held, everywhere.\n- **If a player's pad disconnects mid-class,** pause and say so on screen.\n- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\n- **Audio on a controller ✔.** Call `AudioContext.resume()` on pad input too, not only on keys and clicks. My first wiring left a controller-only player in silence. I can't confirm here that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio, so if the context is still suspended a few seconds after pad input, show a small tag asking for a key or click.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The balance model\n\nThis is the heart of the game and it is the most tested part of this plan. Copy it, then tune it.\n\n**The doll's \"balance core\"** is one Rapier dynamic body on a ball joint at the foot. Everything in the game that stands uses it. Its colliders can carry props, and those props push back on it through real contacts, so a heavy hat makes you top-heavy without any special code.\n\n```js\n// m = 1, h = 0.75 (foot to centre of mass), doll stands 1.5 m tall. Fixed step 1/120 s.\nconst body = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()\n  .setTranslation(fx, fy + 0.75, fz).setCanSleep(false).setAdditionalSolverIterations(4));\nworld.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.capsule(0.5, 0.25).setMass(1).setCollisionGroups(DOLL(lane)), body);\nconst joint = world.createImpulseJoint(\n  RAPIER.JointData.spherical({x:0,y:0,z:0}, {x:0,y:-0.75,z:0}), footBody, body, true);\njoint.setContactsEnabled(false);            // footBody is kinematicPositionBased\n\nconst G = 9.81 * 0.75, I = 0.75 * 0.75 + 0.06;   // gravity moment, inertia about the foot\nconst K = G * (1 + margin), wn = Math.sqrt((K - G) / I), D = 2 * zeta * wn * I;\n\n// Each fixed step, before world.step():\nconst r = 1 - Math.exp(-reflex * dt);\nwx += (wishX - wx) * r;  wz += (wishZ - wz) * r;       // wish = the input, on the unit disc\nconst tx = wx * maxLean, tz = wz * maxLean;            // target lean in radians\nconst q = body.rotation();\nconst ux = 2*(q.x*q.y - q.w*q.z), uy = 1 - 2*(q.x*q.x + q.z*q.z), uz = 2*(q.y*q.z + q.w*q.x);\nleanX = Math.atan2(ux, uy);  leanZ = Math.atan2(uz, uy);   // lean toward +x and +z\nconst w = body.angvel();\nlet Tx = -(G*Math.sin(tx) + K*(leanX - tx)) + D*w.z;    // gravity feed-forward, spring, damper\nlet Tz = -(G*Math.sin(tz) + K*(leanZ - tz)) - D*w.x;\nconst limit = ankleMax * (flailLeft > 0 ? flailBoost : 1);\nconst m = Math.hypot(Tx, Tz);  if (m > limit) { Tx *= limit/m; Tz *= limit/m; }\n// nx, nz are the disturbances (sway, tremor, gusts, shoves), in the same lean space, not clamped\nbody.resetTorques(true);\nbody.addTorque({ x: Tz + nz, y: -w.y * 0.6, z: -(Tx + nx) }, true);\n```\n\n**Defaults ✔:** `maxLean` 18°, `margin` 0.45, `zeta` 0.40, `ankleMax` 4.6 N·m, `reflex` 10 /s, `flailBoost` 1.9, flail lasts 0.35 s with a 2 s cooldown, fall at 45° of lean.\n\n**What those numbers do ✔:**\n- Full stick from rest reaches 90% in about 1 s, overshoots to about 23° and settles at 18°. It is a soft, wobbly, toy-like feel.\n- Whipping the stick end to end at about 0.5 Hz survives. Slamming it from −18° to +18° peaks near 28° at `ankleMax` 4.2 and up, and falls at 3.8. This is why careful correction matters.\n- The torque limit makes a natural point of no return at `asin(ankleMax / G)`, about 39° with no load. Hat and stone loads lower it automatically.\n\n**Gusts at the defaults ✔ (0.5 s long; these numbers are the difficulty dial):**\n\n| Gust | Do nothing | Flail as you pass about 30° | Stick against it once it has started | Braced: stick at 60% against it, 0.5 s before it hits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 N·m | safe, peaks 22° | | | |\n| 2.5 N·m | safe, peaks 31° | | | |\n| 3 N·m | **falls** | saved, peaks 38° | falls | safe, peaks 19° |\n| 3.5 N·m | falls | saved only if timed near 32°, peaks 43° | falls | safe, peaks 29° |\n| 4 N·m | falls | falls | falls | falls |\n\nSo a telegraph is worth a lot: **give a warning before every gust of 3 N·m or more.** And flail is a real last-moment save with timing in it: a flail used early, before you have moved much, wears off and you still fall. It is the moment people will shout at the TV.\n\n**The load rule ✔.** Every prop adds a gravity moment δ = mass × 9.81 × height. The doll grows unstable when total δ exceeds K − G (3.3 at the default margin). A prop's mass therefore decides how many you can carry. Too heavy and the doll tips over at once: with margin 0.45, 0.06 kg fruit tipped it at the 5th or 6th catch, while 0.015 kg works out to about 13. **Keep prop masses small** (0.01 to 0.03 kg) against the doll's 1 kg.\n\n**Slow sway is what makes holding still a game ✔.** A thumb is far steadier than a body on a board, so a player who simply parks the stick on a target and leaves it scored 100% in my first test. Fast tremor barely moves the dot. The fix is slow forcing that the player has to see and counter by hand. Add it to `nx`, `nz`:\n\n```js\nnx = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.9 + ph)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.73 + ph*2.1)*0.4)\n   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*8.2 + 1.1)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*18.3)*0.4);\nnz = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.77 + ph*1.7)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.51 + ph*0.6)*0.4)\n   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*7.1 + 2.3)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*15.7 + 0.7)*0.4);   // ph from the event's seed\n```\nSway units are N·m. Sway of 0.2 moves the dot about a quarter of the disc if ignored. Tremor is mostly for looks and rumble.\n\n**Traps I hit ✔:**\n- **The doll's colliders must not touch the ground.** The capsule's foot rotating into the floor silently limits lean to about 8° and every event feels dead. Use collision groups: `(membership << 16) | filter`. Bits: `GROUND 0x0001`, props of lane *i* `0x0010 << i`, doll of lane *i* `0x0100 << i`, ragdoll parts `0x1000`. A doll meets only its own lane's props. A prop meets its own lane's doll, its own lane's props and the ground. Ragdoll parts meet only the ground. Lanes never touch each other.\n- **Stacked things use cuboid colliders.** Convex-hull stones collapsed by themselves with no disturbance at all (5th stone, every seed). Plain cuboids held. Draw a rounded stone over a box collider.\n- **Foot motion must be smooth.** With a kinematic foot bobbing 3 cm, or moving in stop-start strides, everything left the tray. Steps set a target speed and the foot low-passes toward it. Bob the visual only.\n- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\n- **A Rapier panic kills the physics for the whole page ✔.** It surfaces as `RuntimeError: unreachable` from the WASM module, and no later call recovers. I hit two causes, both of them easy to write by accident:\n  1. **Touching a body after removing it, or removing it twice.** Drop every reference when you remove a body: remove it from your lists in the same line.\n  2. **Removing a prop and the collider it rested on in the same step, or the support first and the prop later.** `world.step()` panics about half a second later, once the body moves. Even one stone does it. It looks fine if nothing moves, so a quick test can miss it. **Safe: remove the prop, step once, then remove the support.** `collider.setEnabled(false)` and `body.setEnabled(false)` are safe too, and so is freeing the whole world.\n  Everything else the plan does soaked clean ✔: ragdoll spawn and remove 30 times with the doll swaying, four ragdolls at once, fruit loaded and removed 40 times, removing and re-creating the foot joint 30 times, plate shards 40 times, tumblers respawned 30 times, 60 fresh worlds in a row, props removed on a gravity-tilted island. If a panic still happens: out of a session, save and reload the page; in a session, call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Never catch it and carry on.\n\n**The Balance Disc.** `cob = (leanX, leanZ) / maxLean` is the dot, shown truthfully. Draw it as inline SVG and move it with transforms each frame. It is the game's signature HUD: a large circular dial with rings, a target zone that turns green when you are inside, the solid dot, a faint ring showing where your stick is asking to go, and a short inked trail of the last half second, like a seismograph needle. Diameter is about 22% of screen height, bottom left. Trudy peeks over its rim and reacts. In Group Class each player gets a smaller one (14%) along the bottom edge.\n\n**Falling.** At 45° of lean the doll becomes a ragdoll (section 10) with a clatter of wooden clacks, a quip, a rumble and a comic \"CLACK!\" word in the world. After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost. Disable the core while the ragdoll runs (`body.setEnabled(false)`). On recovery reset its pose, velocities and **your cached `leanX`, `leanZ`** ✔: if you forget, the stale lean makes the doll fall again at once, for ever. Falls are counted in the Journal as Floor Exercises.\n\n**Flail.** One button, all devices. Arms windmill for 0.35 s and the ankle gets `flailBoost` strength. Cooldown is 2 s. It looks ridiculous on purpose. It is the save that makes near-falls exciting, and it only works if you time it (see the table above). A flail costs a small form penalty in Stillness events, and the instructor comments.\n\n**Breath.** In Stillness events a breath ring pulses: 4 s growing, 4 s shrinking. Hold Breathe while it grows, release while it shrinks. Over the last full cycle, 70%+ in sync multiplies sway and tremor by 0.6 and form gain by 1.25. Out of sync does nothing bad except miss the bonus. Accessibility toggle: auto-breathe. It is worth a lot ✔: perfectly in sync, Poses Tier 1 goes 95→100 / 66→91 / 54→78, and The Cairn Tier 2 goes from 5.3 / 4.8 / 2 stones of 8 to 8 / 8 / 6.3.\n\n**Difficulty** (Settings): Gentle, Standard (default), Honest. It multiplies `ankleMax` by 1.25 / 1 / 0.9, disturbances by 0.65 / 1 / 1.2 and rhythm windows by 1.4 / 1 / 0.8.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Programme and progress\n\n**Days are game days, not calendar days.** A day ends when you go to bed. The real date is only used for absence jokes and calendar stamps.\n\n| Day | Name | Check-In tests | Classes | What it teaches |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Arrival | Stand Still, Lean Match, Step Time | Poses, Fruit Hat | Lean, flail, breath, the jar |\n| 2 | The Second Day, Which Is Harder | Stand Still, Lean Match, Quick Lean | Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn | Feet, rhythm, stacking |\n| 3 | Service With a Wobble | One-Foot Hold, Lean Match, Step Time | Room Service, Plate Spinners, Plank & Perch | Stepping, stick circles. The Mirror opens |\n| 4 | A Little Damp | Quick Lean, One-Foot Hold, Stand Still | Springboard, Still Water, Island Loop | Air control. Group Class invitation |\n| 5 | Level Day | Your three best tests | Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, The Grand Hold | The ending |\n| 6+ | Maintenance | Random three | Trudy's random plan of three | Free Play continues forever |\n\nBuild the day list from the event registry: an event that was cut (section 18) is skipped and everything still works.\n\n**A day's flow:** morning line → personal question (Yes/No) → Check-In → Wobble Age reveal → Today's Plan (cards in any order: walk there or pick a card to go directly) → each class → Results → marbles → the island comes up a notch → extra Free Play if wanted → the bed hut. Press Use, the screen inks out, a stamp lands on the calendar, the next dawn. Palettes follow the day's steps (dawn, noon, golden, dusk), unless an event pins its own (Step Class at dusk, The Grand Hold at sunset).\n\n**The Check-In** (about 90 s) takes place on a round mat with Trudy. Three tests, each one screen with one big instruction. Each scores 0 to 1 (clamped):\n- **Stand Still** (10 s, sway .1, tremor .06): keep the dot in the centre. Score `1 − meanDistance / .25` ✔ .82 / .74 / .49.\n- **Lean Match** (18 s, sway .04): \"Lean 70% left.\" A bar shows your percentage. Three targets, 6 s each, ignoring the first 1.5 s of each. Score `1 − meanError / .4` ✔ .58 / .45 / .27. (My first version, hold within ±5% for 2 s, was impossible: every bot timed out.)\n- **Step Time** (16 s): alternate feet with a metronome at 96 BPM. Score `1 − meanError / 120 ms` ✔ .81 / .63 / .37.\n- **Quick Lean** (15 s): lean toward the lit quadrant, six prompts. Score `1 − (meanReaction − .35 s) / .9 s` ✔ .87 / .69 / .44.\n- **One-Foot Hold** (12 s): keep R2 down and L2 up, which halves `margin`, while keeping the dot centred. Score as Stand Still ✔ .55 / .48 / .40.\n\n**Wobble Age.** `age = round(18 + 81 × (1 − mean)^1.3 + handicap)`, clamped to 18..99. The handicap is a comic head start that shrinks each day: +24, +16, +8, +3, then 0. So the number always falls, and that is what makes coming back feel good. With unchanged skill, Day 1 to Day 5 ✔: 56→32 / 66→42 / 86→62. The reveal is the comic set piece: lights dim, drum roll, numbers spin on a wooden counter, sting, Trudy's line from a band of lines by age. Stored per day and drawn as the Journal's graph. It never asks your real age (\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\").\n\n**Marbles.** After each class: 2 per minute of active play (min 2) + medal bonus (1, 2, 3, 5) + 2 for a first clear. Each Check-In gives 1. They pour physically into the jar on the island, as real Rapier balls: a 200-marble pour runs fine (✔ 0.23 ms per step; 500 is 0.67 ms). Marble colour shows the medal. Thresholds unlock hats and paints (`src/data/unlocks.js`: 6, 14, 24, 36, 50, 60 marbles). A full programme pays about 15, 37, 59, 81, 103 by the end of each day, or 61 in total with all Tier B events cut, so everything unlocks either way ✔.\n\n**The island's tilt depends on progress, not skill.** Rest tilt = 9° × (1 − completed programme steps / total steps), so nobody can be stuck. Each step lowers it with a long wooden creak, all loose props shuffling and Trudy reacting. The last notch happens inside The Grand Hold.\n\n**Medals** come from form (0 to 100): ≥40 Adequate, ≥65 Pleasant, ≥85 Exemplary, ≥97 Suspicious. Below 40 is still a pass (\"Attended\"). Section 6 gives an event's form where it has its own formula. Everywhere else, form = 100 × raw / `par`, with `par` set per tier so the sharp bot lands 88 to 95 (section 17).\n\n**Tiers.** Every event has three: Gentle, Honest, Unreasonable. Tier 2 opens with a Pleasant on Tier 1, Tier 3 with an Exemplary on Tier 2. Days 1 to 5 use Tier 1. Maintenance days use the highest tier opened. Free Play offers all open tiers.\n\n**Free Play** opens each event once it has been met in the programme. Walk to its pavilion, or use the menu.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat TASK.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat TASK.md'","aggregated_output":"SOURCE: Wii Fit\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-max-20261104-232145].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Use Three.js R180, WEBGPU, use a stylized cel-shaded / ink filter, rapier 0.19.3 for physics, Web Audio, Gamepad API; The rest of the tech stack is yours to choose.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-max-20261104-232145] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-max-20261104-232145].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-04 23:21:45\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort max\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
